WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.180
A gradient can bring an axon to the right neighbourhood.

2
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.820
But a thin layer is either the right one, or it is not.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:16.020
In the retina, each cell type sends its processes into one or a few sublaminae.

4
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.020
How does a process choose?

5
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.520
Discrete choices often call for discrete labels.

6
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:28.053
In chick retina, two proteins, Sidekick-1 and Sidekick-2, are made by largely

7
00:00:28.053 --> 00:00:29.413
separate groups of neurons.

8
00:00:30.093 --> 00:00:33.073
The processes of each group end in different sublaminae.

9
00:00:33.673 --> 00:00:37.613
Each protein binds its own kind, which is called homophilic binding.

10
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:41.953
Cells carrying Sidekick-1 stick to other cells carrying Sidekick-1.

11
00:00:42.683 --> 00:00:47.883
The key test was misexpression, which means making a protein in a cell that normally

12
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:52.563
lacks it. Retinal neurons were made to produce a Sidekick they did not normally

13
00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:57.783
make. Their processes shifted toward the sublamina where that Sidekick was already

14
00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:01.663
concentrated. So a label biases where an arbor ends.

15
00:01:02.203 --> 00:01:04.743
It is one part of how layers form, not the whole.

16
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:06.807
Now the opposite problem.

17
00:01:07.307 --> 00:01:11.647
A neuron must keep its own branches apart, which is called self -avoidance.

18
00:01:12.127 --> 00:01:17.167
In the fly, the protein Dscam1 also binds mainly to identical forms.

19
00:01:17.647 --> 00:01:22.807
But here the outcome is repulsion, so two touching branches with the same forms pull

20
00:01:22.807 --> 00:01:28.767
apart. Without Dscam1, fly sensory neurons have dendrites that cross and bundle with

21
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:29.767
their own branches.

22
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:35.120
To tell itself from its neighbours, a fly sensory neuron needs its own surface

23
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:42.220
identity. A single fly gene, Dscam1, can be spliced into 38 ,016 forms.

24
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.240
Each neuron appears to express a small, partly random subset, and a large menu keeps

25
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.760
accidental matches rare.

26
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.280
Its own branches match, so they pull apart, while branches of other neurons do not

27
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.560
match and can overlap.

28
00:01:57.433 --> 00:02:02.753
Gradients give continuous order, while labels give discrete membership, such as a

29
00:02:02.753 --> 00:02:04.553
layer, or self versus other.

30
00:02:05.073 --> 00:02:07.413
The same chemistry can serve opposite ends.

31
00:02:08.013 --> 00:02:10.313
Matching Sidekick draws processes together.

32
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:13.593
Matching descam one drives branches apart.

33
00:02:14.213 --> 00:02:17.193
What the cell does after the bond forms decides which.
